<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grossmünster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–<a href="/years/1220">1220</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Grossmuenster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grossmünster is a Romanesque church in Zürich, by tradition founded on the graves of the city's patron saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-and-regula">Felix and Regula</a>, with twin towers that are a landmark of the city. Built mainly between about 1100 and 1220 as a collegiate church, it became the centre of the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> was its preacher from 1519.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grossmunster.jpg" alt="Grossmünster" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland zh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grossm%C3%BCnster_-_M%C3%BCnsterhof_2014-05-23_12-08-43.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
